text: <|channel|>analysis<|message|>We need to answer: "When does it get slow?" The user previously asked: "Explain what a hash table is and why lookups are fast." The assistant responded with a description. Now the user asks: "When does it get slow?" So we need to explain scenarios where hash table lookups become slow: collisions, poor hash function, high load factor, rehashing, memory locality, etc. Also mention that worst-case is O(n) if all keys hash to same bucket. Also mention that if the hash table is too full, collisions increase, causing longer chains or probing sequences. Also mention that if the hash function is poor, collisions happen. Also mention that if the table is too small, rehashing overhead. Also mention that if the keys are not well distributed, or if the hash table is not properly sized, or if the underlying array is large causing cache misses. Also mention that if the hash table is used in a multi-threaded environment,
